true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141088/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141088"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ded6d3f3-ed85-50ce-b37c-23927cf96273" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "414b32a6-1f12-5749-a310-b2a4bae616dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "17c7effc-7e27-5184-8825-b4c6d7d39a18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "cd36af1f-6913-5e4a-9a85-c5c91ea87e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6d65cf3f-748a-5527-9dc5-7b5ea788c014" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "727d3302-be7f-55b9-87be-dc2fd7e7331d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141081/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-141081/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141081/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-141081/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141081/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141081/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141081" :question/id #uuid "ed1c0185-bf53-5e0e-8f4f-d5c9ea9f7c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-141081" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141081/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-141078"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "9f50fb7e-7563-5542-8980-5976cd65691d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "90bc8aa8-bab3-5613-9eac-d17417da671d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e07d4240-42d1-58a5-90cd-5ebfeb0eec55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f06491ec-ec91-52e1-a937-106058d39f8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "7db58664-e7cc-5cc9-a75a-f8e43152cbbf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "28919156-568a-5af2-bd1e-f2edbd4e929c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141084/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer-field/choices ["q-141084/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141084/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-141084/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141084/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141084/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141084" :question/id #uuid "dd7f154e-bda2-5ad0-9a82-1b52f8f224b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141084/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141084"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "22c8ebdf-8e74-5323-9672-5c39508def7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "582b77a2-82b2-521f-aa21-1eea403a5828" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "27b529fd-6543-558f-9aeb-7d399d8bf7da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d8eb959c-4147-5c9f-87ec-004b09f3501f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "625d9350-740f-5374-b637-d8e7c15778af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "75912ee8-4ef3-5ab6-ad3b-7e543bef68c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141099/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-141099/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141099/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141099/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141099/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141099/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141099" :question/id #uuid "06bd8f05-4440-5830-a789-d3d0eb85823a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141099/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141099"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "dfdbd113-4623-5509-8fdc-b2a5abf2ac68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fbc4271d-fc42-5c47-925e-47c5a6af43d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "22f67456-a8be-5899-a53b-6c7238a127bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "688e2f89-72c9-5bc4-bdae-2e86d57afead" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e8973378-592a-594c-aa5e-008a56a47abd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f39c88af-1ce8-5878-b920-e2ef2b2843f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141098/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer-field/choices ["q-141098/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141098/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141098/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141098/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-141098/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141098" :question/id #uuid "d10faf2f-cc2e-5e28-a752-c6203b812185" :question/math-academy-id "q-141098" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=4x$\n- $y=2{x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(4x)}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\ne 16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y=4x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(2{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}8x \\\\ -4x & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ne 4{x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & \\ne {x}^{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141098/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141098"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8eda923f-1829-5e8c-88f7-3943b21e23fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "80ce65d4-a0f0-5dcf-a74a-2943f8ee9d4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "edadaffd-dff1-5ac4-bcbd-3ab12dd79405" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "47d9e7d0-433c-50c0-b8b8-873dc870e499" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e99ba2b9-eed2-57d8-8720-a71caa9e2f60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d70d9c7f-1be6-57d6-9f0a-ad943b684745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141103/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer-field/choices ["q-141103/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-141103/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141103/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-141103/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-141103/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141103" :question/id #uuid "482773e5-5b8a-5acd-8d88-a970f8a254f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141103" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141103/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141103"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "24d20f25-8bb9-5064-94e6-5bca9c6df55b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "91627b8a-627f-5c2e-a6ff-0ff3d52ce635" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c3f7892e-2376-5f4d-ab2f-2fc9850117bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1aea66b2-c4cb-5cf9-b060-bc56cab96f27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c58e99f4-4cc4-5dd5-b7e6-5456542cb904" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "14ce3d7f-290b-5336-8578-d6aa19601a4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141093/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-141093/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141093/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141093/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141093/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141093/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141093" :question/id #uuid "78b7d940-8463-5444-b751-5735936b4729" :question/math-academy-id "q-141093" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141093/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79335"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-352655/field-1/88b4166623f106aacf166d8bce409762bb41c6b21aedbca826b581949369954c" :answer/id #uuid "8cf997eb-6058-5c08-a5f0-d2d144aba426" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352655/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "07c72c27-a9d0-5f4d-9333-493f99092f6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352655/field-1/88b4166623f106aacf166d8bce409762bb41c6b21aedbca826b581949369954c" :answer-field/choices ["q-352655/field-1/88b4166623f106aacf166d8bce409762bb41c6b21aedbca826b581949369954c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352655" :question/id #uuid "53d754a0-a4b9-5c1a-ac3a-5eea26512dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352655" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352655/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352655"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "215ceeea-487c-5f40-9245-2583305a05c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "bb422677-19d4-5aa1-b131-994d01547210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection/5a1fe28b022029f27455dc36a9cc0dbfd0eced1186649091706824f56db6ba8d" :answer/id #uuid "35e8bf6b-ce33-5506-9a77-254f5debf40e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection/72443840a1994ef4e387277621cd8f464de0132f9c0b12ec6eac8b3cee6eca7d" :answer/id #uuid "3235756d-a5e8-5e0d-a321-bbfddf3c263c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb" :answer/id #uuid "6ed42e76-aefd-5fe2-8627-165a1f88ee6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "24ce5546-027f-5aa5-8d0b-ae5b485b1d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141115/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb" :answer-field/choices ["q-141115/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-141115/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-141115/selection/5a1fe28b022029f27455dc36a9cc0dbfd0eced1186649091706824f56db6ba8d" "q-141115/selection/72443840a1994ef4e387277621cd8f464de0132f9c0b12ec6eac8b3cee6eca7d" "q-141115/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141115" :question/id #uuid "e96414b2-2fe9-598e-bc15-f2c64a7b0633" :question/math-academy-id "q-141115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x) \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141115/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141115"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347839/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "6fa1aea7-e4fc-55eb-bf43-73fc9cc402bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347839/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cecf1b03-c117-5235-b1b5-08aac2a9d69f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347839/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-347839/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347839" :question/id #uuid "ca945874-8c01-5627-9fbe-551ce7abcbd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-347839" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-347839/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347839"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352661/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "9301ecaa-5562-59d2-9824-03d89e8e9091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352661/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "705c0757-8bae-50e6-b0e1-3052e076c7da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352661/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f624629-f247-5f0c-aec0-1a562323566d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352661/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-352661/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-352661/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352661" :question/id #uuid "a23d5159-a605-5e2a-a3e7-d983f61a07c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352661" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A-x} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A-0} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352661/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352649/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "800f6c63-9feb-5808-823d-56b953be91a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352649/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac021c72-523a-518b-bf91-d26f2726c50f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352649/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-352649/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352649" :question/id #uuid "598a1832-7b44-5120-9380-666c04a91eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-352649" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =Acos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352649/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352649"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-525"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9978"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8732"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
